Use `pg` (node-postgres) with `Pool`:

```javascript
import { Pool } from 'pg';

const pool = new Pool({
  connectionString: process.env.DATABASE_URL,
  // Pool sizing
  max: 20,                    // max connections
  min: 2,                     // keep alive minimum
  idleTimeoutMillis: 30_000,  // kill idle conn after 30s
  connectionTimeoutMillis: 5_000, // fail fast if no conn available
  // Query-level timeout (kills long-running queries)
  statement_timeout: 10_000,
  query_timeout: 10_000,
});

// Catch idle errors (e.g. DB restart kills conn)
pool.on('error', (err) => {
  console.error('Idle client error:', err);
});

// Wrapper — always releases
export async function query(text, params) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    const res = await client.query(text, params);
    return res;
  } finally {
    client.release();
  }
}

// Graceful shutdown
process.on('SIGTERM', async () => {
  await pool.end();
});
```

Key points:

| Config | Why |
|--------|-----|
| `max: 20` | Match DB's `max_connections` minus headroom for other apps |
| `idleTimeoutMillis` | Reclaim unused conns; prevents stale socket buildup |
| `connectionTimeoutMillis` | Surface pool exhaustion fast instead of hanging |
| `statement_timeout` | Kill runaway queries at DB level, not just app level |
| `pool.on('error')` | Without this, unhandled rejection crashes process |

For transactions, use `client` directly and always release in `finally`. Never let transaction clients escape without release.
